Due to their competitive advantages, such as options for structural customization, functionalizable porosity, and high crystallinity, proton conductors derived from multifunctional metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have drawn rapidly increased attention in the areas of solid-state electrolytes, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ sensors, separation and purification of $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ (de)hydrogenation of organic compounds, and ammonia synthesis.[1–3] However, these proton conductors typically work only at high temperature with high relative humidity (RH) or in an anhydrous
The density of the hydrogen-bonding (H-bonding) network formed inside the framework is one of the key factors for the mobility of protons or proton carriers. Although oxalate-based MOFs are rich of oxygen atoms that favor the formation of H-bonding networks, and such MOFs with remarkable proton conductivity performances at room temperature have been reported, these MOFs suffer from poor water stability.[21–23] Meanwhile, lanthanide MOFs have shown excellent water stability, but typically with only average room-temperature proton conductivities $(10^{-5}–10^{-3}~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{~cm^{-1}})$ .[24–27] Inspiringly, several hydrazinium lanthanide oxalates with good water stability have recently been constructed.[28] Considering that hydrazinium can serve as a proton source, we reason that high proton conductivity and good water stability can be simultaneously realized in lanthanide–oxalate MOFs.
Moreover, if the characteristic RH-responsive luminescence properties of lanthanide–oxalate MOFs are further combined,[27–32] not only the synthesis of multifunctional MOFs that are water stable, luminescent, and with decent proton conductivity can be realized, but also the indirect relationship of proton conductivity versus luminescence intensity can be determined by correlating the relationships of RH versus proton conductivity and RH versus luminescence intensity. Utilizing this relationship, the electric signal of proton conductivity will be readily translated to the optical signal of luminescence intensity, which will then be directly monitored by the naked eye. Herein, for the first time, we synthesized a lanthanide–oxalate compound MOF-1, $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})[\mathrm{CeEu}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})]\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ that 1) has the highest room-temperature proton conductivity $(3.42\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ under $100\%$ RH among lanthanide-based MOFs,[24,25] 2) generates strong red $(618~\mathrm{nm})$ luminescence emission that varies with RH under ultraviolet (UV) excitation $(394~\mathrm{nm})$ , and 3) is remarkabley water stable.
As illustrated in Scheme 1, with a gradual increase of RH from left to right, the density of the H-bonding network increases, leading to a continuous increase of the proton conductivity within a wide RH range $(20-100\%)$ ). Concomitantly, the intensity of the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ luminescence emission at $618~\mathrm{nm}$ continuously dims. More importantly, the relationship between proton conductivity and luminescence intensity can be intrinsically correlated by the RH, providing a robust and dual-channel material for potential humidity sensing. To further widen the working photoluminescence wavelength of this concept, $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})[\mathrm{Nd}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})]\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , a second compound MOF-2 that generates strong near infrared (NIR) emission $(1063~\mathrm{nm})$ under NIR $\left(808\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ excitation, has the second-highest proton conductivity $(2.70\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ among reported lanthanidebased MOFs, and decent water stability, was also synthesized.
Due to the high similarity of the coordination behavior of the lanthanide elements, the absolute positions of ${\mathrm{Ce}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in the framework could not be authentically determined. However, MOF-1 was synthesized by intentionally setting the molar ratio of $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}{:}\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ at 1:1 in the reaction mixture. Consistently, the sequential inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectroscopy result of the as-grown single crystals gave a $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}{:}\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ molar ratio of 1.04, which is close to the theoretical value of 1 (Table S1, Supporting Information). MOF-1 exhibits crystal lattice (Figure 1a,b) that belongs to the monoclinic space group $C2/m$ , as confirmed by both single-crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD) and powder XRD (PXRD), as shown in Table S2 and Figure S1a in the Supporting Information, respectively. Three oxalate ligands exhibit two different coordination modes, i.e., ligand ox1 possesses a $\mu_{3}$ -bridging mode while ligands ox2 and ox3 display a $\mu_{2}$ -bridging mode (Figure 1c–e).
As seen in Figure 1f,g, the hexagonal honeycomb mono­ layer is parallel to the crystallographic ab plane. Two mono­ layers are fused into a bilayer connected by a neighboring $\mathrm{LnO_{9}}$ polyhedron, where two $\mathrm{LnO_{9}}$ polyhedra are shared by a $\eta^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}(1){-}\eta^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}(1)$ edge and the honeycomb-like bilayers are linked into a 3D pillared-layer-type open framework along the $c$ -axis. The structures of two fused hexagonal polyhedral rings of the framework in different monolayers viewed from the $c-$ , $a\cdot$ -, and $b$ -axes are depicted in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). Notably, as illustrated in Figure S3 (Supporting Information), quite short interatomic distances $(<3.0\mathrm{~\AA})$ exist between the donors and acceptors of the H-bonding network, which are found between the oxalate ligands in the framework and the lattice water molecules/hydraziniums, between the coordinated hydraziniums and the lattice water molecules/noncoordinated hydraziniums, and between the lattice water molecules and the noncoordinated hydraziniums. These short interatomic distances indicate that a very dense H-bonding network is formed in the crystal lattice of MOF-1, implying great potential for high proton conductivity.
The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images in Figure 2a–d show the morphologies of MOF-1 immersed in water at room temperature for 0, 5, 10, and $15\mathrm{~d~}$ where no obvious physical morphology variation was found after careful comparison of the corresponding images. Furthermore, the PXRD patterns in Figure 2e display almost identical diffraction peaks, suggesting that from an atomic position point of view, the characteristic crystalline nature of the MOF-1 framework was maintained. The above two results indicate that the sample was not affected after being soaked in water for over 2 weeks, i.e., MOF-1 has fairly good water stability.
To determine the proton conductivity, alternating current (AC) impedance spectra of MOF-1 at $298\substack{-313\mathrm{~K~}}$ and under $100\%$ RH were collected in the frequency region of $10^{2}{\mathrm{-}}10^{6}~\mathrm{Hz}$ . The proton conductivity was calculated by fitting this arc at selected temperatures using the ZView program, where the resistance includes two parts, i.e., $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ , representing the resistance from the bulk phase and the grain boundaries, respectively, as depicted in Figure $\mathtt{S4a}$ in the Supporting
Information.[5,33] It should be noted that the total resistance, the summary of $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ was utilized for the calculation of proton conductivity. The characteristic Nyquist plot of MOF-1 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under $100\%$ RH is shown in Figure 3a, where the calculated proton conductivity $(\sigma)$ is $3.42\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , indicating highly efficient proton conduction even at room temperature. The Nyquist plots of MOF-1 at other temperatures are shown in Figure S4b–e (Supporting Information) where $\sigma$ continuously increases to $3.68\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $303\ \mathrm{K},$ $4.08\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $308\ \mathrm{K},$ and finally to $4.80\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at 313 K. This is consistent with the typical proton conducting behavior.
Although MOF-based proton conductors have been widely investigated over the last decade, only a small number of these materials contained lanthanide elements and only a few showed high room-temperature proton conductivity and the highest record obtained is $1.3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ (Table S4, Supporting Information).[24–26,32–38] To the best of our knowledge, MOF-1 discussed in this work displays so far the best room-temperature proton conductivity of 3.42 × 10−3 S cm−1 and possesses an added benefit of remarkable water stability, which is due to the high coordination number of the lanthanide ions introduced in the framework.
The linear relationship of $\ln(\sigma T)$ versus $1000/T$ plotted in the inset of Figure 3a is highly consistent with the Arrhenius equation, allowing the proton-transport activation energy, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ , to be calculated from the temperature-dependent conductivity data collected between 298 and $313\mathrm{~K~}$ :
$$
\ln(\sigma T)=\ln A-{\frac{E_{\mathrm{{a}}}}{k_{\mathrm{{B}}}T}}
$$
Here $\sigma$ is the proton conductivity, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is the proton-transport activation energy, $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is temperature, and $A$ is a pre-exponential factor. The best fit provided a rather low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $0.10\ \mathrm{eV},$ implying that the Grotthuss mechanism, i.e., proton hopping, along the formed H-bonding network inside the MOF-1 channels, dominates the transport process within this temperature range.
The room-temperature AC impedance spectra under various RHs were further measured, and the RH-dependent Nyquist plots in Figure S6 (Supporting Information) indicate that the proton-transport capability of MOF-1 is quite sensitive to RH variations. The proton transport capability improved dramatically from $1.42{\overset{-}{\times}}10^{-7}{\mathrm{~S~cm}}{\overset{-}{-}}1$ under $28\%$ RH to $2.29\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ under $45\%$ , $1.37\times10^{-5}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaS~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ under $60\%$ RH, $8.30\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ under $75\%$ RH, and finally $3.42\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ under $100\%$ RH. Such a large variation with a magnitude range of $10^{4}$ implies the easy manipulability of MOF-1 proton conductivity by simply controlling the RH. The entire conductivity of a single-phase conductor can be described as:
$$
\scriptstyle\sigma=\sum n_{i}q_{i}\mu_{i}
$$
where $n_{i},\ q_{i},$ and $\mu_{i}$ represent the concentration, charge, and mobility of the ith carrier, respectively.[4] Although the proton (or charge-carrier) concentration was not significantly improved under different RHs, the proton mobility was enhanced under higher RH owing to the formation of a higher-density H-bonding network, i.e., more proton-transport pathways, corresponding to a greater than four orders of magnitude increase of proton conductivity are formed as the RH increases from $28\%$ to $100\%$ .
The water-adsorption isotherm measurement in Figure 3b indicates that the amount of adsorbed water increases at elevated RHs. The highest water content reaches $81.26~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the maximum allowable RH of $100\%$ . No deliquesce occurring during the entire measurement suggests again the good water stability of MOF-1, which also agrees with the results drawn from Figure 2. The density of the H-bonding network dramatically increases and a higher number of pathways are produced compared to that under low RH (see also Scheme 1), which results in an enormously improved proton conductivity. On the other hand, the gradually increased amount of adsorbed water under increased RHs in Figure 3b also agrees very well with the continuous increase of the proton conductivity shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information).
Figure 4a shows the room-temperature luminescence emission spectra of MOF-1 under various RHs, where the typical emission profiles at 582, 618, and $692~\mathrm{nm}$ originate from the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to $^7\mathrm{F}_{1},\ ^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to $^7\mathrm{F}_{2}$ , and ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to $^7\mathrm{F}_{4}$ transitions of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ as shown in Figure S7a (Supporting Information), respectively. Emissions of ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to ${}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0}$ and ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to $^7\mathrm{F}_{3}$ transitions were also observed (Figure $S8\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-c}$ , Supporting Information), although relatively weak. Notably, the observed features of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ emission such as the degeneracy of each transition and relatively strong $\mathrm{~D}_{0}$ to $^7\mathrm{F}_{2}$ transition, were well in consistence with the local symmetry of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ obtained by single-crystal XRD.[39] The intensities of the characteristic luminescence emissions of the lanthanide ions in the framework are typically unaffected by their surrounding coordination environments because the typical 4f–4f electron transitions are shielded by the exterior 5s and 5p orbitals. However, in our case, luminescence intensity of the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ inside the frameworks gradually decreased with the increase of RH (Figure 4a). The relationship between the luminescence intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , represented by the red emission at $618~\mathrm{nm}$ and the amount of water inside the crystal lattice, i.e., RH, is plotted as the blue line in Figure 4b. The intensity of the red emission at $618~\mathrm{nm}$ linearly varies with the RH and experiences a massive decrease $(\approx88\%)$ upon increasing the RH from $28\%$ to $100\%$ . The decrease of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ luminescence should be caused by the energy transfer between $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ and $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups of water molecules adsorbed inside the inner and secondary coordination spheres of the framework,[40–43] which was proved by the decreased lifetime of the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ state at high RH (Figure S8d, Supporting Information).
More intriguingly, the proton conductivity of MOF-1 in the form of $\log(\sigma)$ increases linearly with RH as shown in the green line in Figure 4b. Considering the linear relationships of both the luminescence intensity and $\log(\sigma)$ versus RH, the indirect relationship between the luminescence intensity and $\log(\sigma)$ is further plotted as the red line in Figure 4c where the luminescence intensity linearly decreases with an increase of the proton conductivity. This correlation entirely translates the electric signal of proton conductivity to the optical signal of luminescence intensity, which can be easily and directly observed by the naked eye.
A proof-of-concept device made of a thin pellet of MOF-1 was fabricated to measure the RH-dependent proton conductivity and luminescence intensity. The digital photos of this device under different RHs are shown in Figure 4d and indeed, the observed luminescence is gradually weakened at increased RHs. This reflects the variations of the physical activities inside the crystalline framework that otherwise cannot be seen by the naked eye if no luminescent $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ is introduced. By simply observing the digital photos or the color coordinates of the luminescent MOF-1 (Figure 4d; Figure S9, Supporting Information), one can easily tell the variation of RH and proton conductivity.
To diversify the working wavelength of such a dualfunctional material, MOF-2, $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})[\mathrm{Nd}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was further synthesized. The PXRD data in Figure S1b (Supporting Information) confirm the same crystal structure as MOF-1 while Figure S10 (Supporting Information) indicates good water stability and Figure S11a (Supporting Information) proves the outstanding room-temperature proton conductivity $(2.70\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ under $100\%$ RH and low activation energy (0.11 eV), which also correspond very well to the wateradsorption results in Figure S11b (Supporting Information). The room-temperature Nyquist plots in Figure S12 (Supporting Information) show the step-by-step increment of proton conductivity at increased RHs. The luminescence characterization in Figure S13 (Supporting Information) shows a strong NIR emission at $1063\mathrm{nm}$ , which is originated from the $^4\mathrm{F}_{3/2}$ to $^4\mathrm{I}_{11/2}$ energy transition of ${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ under NIR $(808\mathrm{nm})$ ) excitation and following the energy-transfer pathway shown in Figure S7b (Supporting Information). The linear relationships of RH versus the luminescence intensity (blue line in Figure S13b in the Supporting Information), RH versus the proton conductivity in the form of $\log(\sigma)$ (green line in Figure S13b in the Supporting Information), and ultimately the luminescence intensity versus $\log(\sigma)$ are also plotted (red line in Figure S13b in the Supporting Information). These observations further indicate not only the successful translation of the electric signal of proton conductivity to the optical signal of luminescence emission but also the dual-channel humidity sensing capability at a different working wavelength, i.e., $1063~\mathrm{nm}$ .
In summary, we have synthesized two lanthanide-oxalate MOFs with high water-stability, the highest reported room-temperature proton conductivity among lanthanide-based MOFs and most importantly, RH-responsive luminescence emission at multiple working wavelengths. These characteristics allowed for variations of proton conductivity to be readily translated into luminescence intensity changes, which were easily visualized by the naked eye instead of tedious laboratory measurements. Alternatively, the simultaneous response of both the proton conductivity and the luminescence intensity to RH makes these MOF materials promising candidates to fabricate dual-channel humidity sensors or even portable ones. We also believe that better-performance multifunctional MOFs composed of other luminescent lanthanide or transition-metal ions can be synthesized, which is an ongoing project in our laboratory.
